<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gong Zhiqi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gong Zhiqi was a minister of the small state of Yu in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a> in the 7th century BCE. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-xian-of-jin">Duke Xian of Jin</a> asked for passage through Yu to attack Guo, he warned that 'when the lips are gone, the teeth are cold', since Yu and Guo depended on each other. His advice was ignored, and Jin destroyed Guo and then Yu in 655 BCE; his words became a Chinese proverb.</p></div>
